00 North Cramer Street, Milwaukee, WI, 53211, USA."}]}}, {"fullName": "Hasan K", "lastName": "Hasan", "firstName": "Khaled", "initials": "K", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee, College of Engineering & Applied Science, 3200 North Cramer Street, Milwaukee, WI, 53211, USA."}]}}, {"fullName": "Sung Y", "lastName": "Sung", "firstName": "Yongjin", "initials": "Y", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee, College of Engineering & Applied Science, 3200 North Cramer Street, Milwaukee, WI, 53211, USA. Electronic address: ysung4@uwm.edu."}]}}]}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "Pt C", "volume": "312", "journalIssueId": 4285697, "dateOfPublication": "2027 Feb", "monthOfPublication": 2, "yearOfPublication": 2027, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Talanta", "title": "Talanta", "essn": "1873-3573", "issn": "0039-9140", "nlmid": "2984816R", "isoabbreviation": "Talanta"}, "printPublicationDate": "2027-02-01"}, "pubYear": "2027", "pageInfo": "130550", "abstractText": "Short-wave infrared hyperspectral imaging (SWIR-HSI) enables rapid, non-destructive material characterization with high potential for process analytical technology and pharmaceutical screening. Here, we demonstrate high-throughput, full-field SWIR-HSI using Fourier transform spectroscopy (FTS) coupled with deep learning. Conventional FTS typically requires thousands of sampling points over a wide optical path difference (OPD) range to resolve spectral features. By directly processing raw interferograms with deep learning, bypassing conventional Fourier transformation, we achieved 98.9% pixel-level and 100% whole-tablet classification accuracy across 11 over-the-counter pharmaceutical products using only 200 data points acquired over a 10 μm mirror displacement (20 μm OPD). Under challenging independent-lot, multi-day testing conditions, the framework maintained 96.7% pixel-level and 100% whole-tablet accuracy. Latent-space visualizations confirmed that short-range interferograms retain sufficient chemical and physical information for rapid, robust pharmaceutical classification. These findings indicate that deep-learned short-range interferometry offers a promising, vibration-resilient approach for high-throughput, non-destructive quality control of solid dosage pharmaceuticals.", "affiliation": "University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee, College of Engineering & Applied Science, 3200 North Cramer Street, Milwaukee, WI, 53211, USA.", "publicationStatus": "ppublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Print-Electronic", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["Journal Article"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee", "orderIn": 0}]}, "meshHeadingList": {"meshHeading": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Pharmaceutical Preparations", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "CL", "qualifierName": "classification"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "CH", "qualifierName": "chemistry"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "Tablets", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "CL", "qualifierName": "classification"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "CH", "qualifierName": "chemistry"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Spectroscopy, Fourier Transform Infrared", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "MT", "qualifierName": "methods"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "Interferometry", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "MT", "qualifierName": "methods"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Fourier Analysis"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "High-Throughput Screening Assays", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "MT", "qualifierName": "methods"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorN